    "steps": [
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 205,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 206,
        "char_start": 0,
        "effective_step": 1,
        "end_token_index": 61,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 62,
        "step_number": 1,
        "text": "Step 1: To find the minimum value of \\(x^4 + 4y^2 + 4z^4\\) given that \\(xyz = 2\\), we can use the method of Lagrange multipliers or apply inequalities. Here, we will use the AM-GM inequality for simplicity."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 350,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 351,
        "char_start": 208,
        "effective_step": 2,
        "end_token_index": 139,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 140,
        "step_number": 2,
        "text": "Step 2: Apply the AM-GM inequality to the expression \\(x^4 + 4y^2 + 4z^4\\):\n\\[\nx^4 + 4y^2 + 4z^4 \\geq 3 \\sqrt[3]{x^4 \\cdot 4y^2 \\cdot 4z^4}.\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 480,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 481,
        "char_start": 353,
        "effective_step": 3,
        "end_token_index": 201,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 202,
        "step_number": 3,
        "text": "Step 3: Simplify the right-hand side of the inequality:\n\\[\n3 \\sqrt[3]{x^4 \\cdot 4y^2 \\cdot 4z^4} = 3 \\sqrt[3]{16x^4 y^2 z^4}.\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 671,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 672,
        "char_start": 483,
        "effective_step": 4,
        "end_token_index": 294,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 295,
        "step_number": 4,
        "text": "Step 4: Substitute \\(xyz = 2\\) into the expression inside the cube root:\n\\[\n3 \\sqrt[3]{16(xyz)^4} = 3 \\sqrt[3]{16 \\cdot 2^4} = 3 \\sqrt[3]{16 \\cdot 16} = 3 \\sqrt[3]{256} = 3 \\cdot 4 = 12.\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 782,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 783,
        "char_start": 674,
        "effective_step": 5,
        "end_token_index": 337,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 338,
        "step_number": 5,
        "text": "Step 5: Check if this minimum value can be achieved. For equality in AM-GM, we need:\n\\[\nx^4 = 4y^2 = 4z^4.\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 976,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 978,
        "char_start": 785,
        "effective_step": 6,
        "end_token_index": 424,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 425,
        "step_number": 6,
        "text": "Step 6: Solve these equations simultaneously. From \\(x^4 = 4y^2\\), we get \\(y^2 = \\frac{x^4}{4}\\) or \\(y = \\frac{x^2}{2}\\). Similarly, from \\(4y^2 = 4z^4\\), we get \\(y^2 = z^4\\) or \\(y = z^2\\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1094,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1095,
        "char_start": 980,
        "effective_step": 7,
        "end_token_index": 478,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 479,
        "step_number": 7,
        "text": "Step 7: Equate the two expressions for \\(y\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{2} = z^2 \\implies x^2 = 2z^2 \\implies x = \\sqrt{2}z.\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1301,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1302,
        "char_start": 1097,
        "effective_step": 8,
        "end_token_index": 580,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 581,
        "step_number": 8,
        "text": "Step 8: Substitute \\(x = \\sqrt{2}z\\) and \\(y = z^2\\) into the constraint \\(xyz = 2\\):\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{2}z)(z^2)(z) = 2 \\implies \\sqrt{2}z^4 = 2 \\implies z^4 = \\sqrt{2} \\implies z = (\\sqrt{2})^{1/4} = 2^{1/8}.\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1460,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1461,
        "char_start": 1304,
        "effective_step": 9,
        "end_token_index": 672,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 673,
        "step_number": 9,
        "text": "Step 9: Calculate \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) using \\(z = 2^{1/8}\\):\n\\[\nx = \\sqrt{2} \\cdot 2^{1/8} = 2^{1/2} \\cdot 2^{1/8} = 2^{5/8},\n\\]\n\\[\ny = (2^{1/8})^2 = 2^{1/4}.\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1650,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1651,
        "char_start": 1463,
        "effective_step": 10,
        "end_token_index": 794,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 795,
        "step_number": 10,
        "text": "Step 10: Verify the values:\n\\[\nx^4 = (2^{5/8})^4 = 2^{5/2} = 4\\sqrt{2},\n\\]\n\\[\n4y^2 = 4(2^{1/4})^2 = 4 \\cdot 2^{1/2} = 4\\sqrt{2},\n\\]\n\\[\n4z^4 = 4(2^{1/8})^4 = 4 \\cdot 2^{1/2} = 4\\sqrt{2}.\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1744,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1745,
        "char_start": 1653,
        "effective_step": 11,
        "end_token_index": 851,
        "near_end": true,
        "prefix_token_count": 852,
        "step_number": 11,
        "text": "Step 11: Sum these values:\n\\[\nx^4 + 4y^2 + 4z^4 = 4\\sqrt{2} + 4\\sqrt{2} + 4\\sqrt{2} = 12.\n\\]"
      }
    ]
