  "raw_text": "Step 1: To find the minimum value of \\(x^4 + 4y^2 + 4z^4\\) given that \\(xyz = 2\\), we can use the method of Lagrange multipliers or apply inequalities. Here, we will use the AM-GM inequality for simplicity.\n\nStep 2: Apply the AM-GM inequality to the expression \\(x^4 + 4y^2 + 4z^4\\):\n\\[\nx^4 + 4y^2 + 4z^4 \\geq 3 \\sqrt[3]{x^4 \\cdot 4y^2 \\cdot 4z^4}.\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify the right-hand side of the inequality:\n\\[\n3 \\sqrt[3]{x^4 \\cdot 4y^2 \\cdot 4z^4} = 3 \\sqrt[3]{16x^4 y^2 z^4}.\n\\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute \\(xyz = 2\\) into the expression inside the cube root:\n\\[\n3 \\sqrt[3]{16(xyz)^4} = 3 \\sqrt[3]{16 \\cdot 2^4} = 3 \\sqrt[3]{16 \\cdot 16} = 3 \\sqrt[3]{256} = 3 \\cdot 4 = 12.\n\\]\n\nStep 5: Check if this minimum value can be achieved. For equality in AM-GM, we need:\n\\[\nx^4 = 4y^2 = 4z^4.\n\\]\n\nStep 6: Solve these equations simultaneously. From \\(x^4 = 4y^2\\), we get \\(y^2 = \\frac{x^4}{4}\\) or \\(y = \\frac{x^2}{2}\\). Similarly, from \\(4y^2 = 4z^4\\), we get \\(y^2 = z^4\\) or \\(y = z^2\\).\n\nStep 7: Equate the two expressions for \\(y\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{2} = z^2 \\implies x^2 = 2z^2 \\implies x = \\sqrt{2}z.\n\\]\n\nStep 8: Substitute \\(x = \\sqrt{2}z\\) and \\(y = z^2\\) into the constraint \\(xyz = 2\\):\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{2}z)(z^2)(z) = 2 \\implies \\sqrt{2}z^4 = 2 \\implies z^4 = \\sqrt{2} \\implies z = (\\sqrt{2})^{1/4} = 2^{1/8}.\n\\]\n\nStep 9: Calculate \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) using \\(z = 2^{1/8}\\):\n\\[\nx = \\sqrt{2} \\cdot 2^{1/8} = 2^{1/2} \\cdot 2^{1/8} = 2^{5/8},\n\\]\n\\[\ny = (2^{1/8})^2 = 2^{1/4}.\n\\]\n\nStep 10: Verify the values:\n\\[\nx^4 = (2^{5/8})^4 = 2^{5/2} = 4\\sqrt{2},\n\\]\n\\[\n4y^2 = 4(2^{1/4})^2 = 4 \\cdot 2^{1/2} = 4\\sqrt{2},\n\\]\n\\[\n4z^4 = 4(2^{1/8})^4 = 4 \\cdot 2^{1/2} = 4\\sqrt{2}.\n\\]\n\nStep 11: Sum these values:\n\\[\nx^4 + 4y^2 + 4z^4 = 4\\sqrt{2} + 4\\sqrt{2} + 4\\sqrt{2} = 12.\n\\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{12}<|im_end|>",
